# .gitattributes — line-ending + binary policy for the whole repository
# (Unown's own layer AND the vendored pi-repo/, which is tracked in this repo).
#
# Convention: LF everywhere, on every OS.
#   - *.sh is bash-native: LF is mandatory.
#   - *.cmd and *.ps1 stay LF ON PURPOSE. bin/unown.cmd works because it is a
#     label-free single-liner (goto + LF is a cmd.exe parser trap — see the
#     header of bin/unown.cmd), and PowerShell 5.1+ parses LF .ps1 without
#     complaint. CRLF here would be pure churn.
#   - *.bat is kept CRLF: batch files may use labels/goto, where cmd.exe is
#     only reliable with CRLF (pi-repo/pi-test.bat is the one tracked .bat).
#   - `* text=auto eol=lf` lets git binary-sniff (NUL bytes) as a safety net
#     beneath the explicit binary list, and keeps Windows checkouts LF so the
#     repo behaves identically on all three OSes.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh text eol=lf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Windows launchers: LF by design (see the header comment above).
*.cmd text eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=lf

# Binaries — never normalize, never diff.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.node binary
*.deb binary
*.tar binary
*.tar.gz binary
*.tgz binary
*.wasm binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary

# Vendored OS-sandbox binary (ELF, no extension) — keep git's hands off it.
# (Defense in depth: the vendored pi-repo ships its own .gitattributes that
# governs its subtree — for this path it relies on `text=auto` NUL-sniffing,
# which keeps the ELF untouched; this line covers us if that ever changes.)
pi-repo/packages/self-learning/native/bin/bwrap binary

# Vendored mermaid dist (gate-15 renderer, sha-pinned in mermaid_check.mjs) —
# generated code: excluded from language stats, excluded from text diffs.
scripts/vendor/mermaid-*.min.js linguist-generated=true -diff
